- op: discuss  # a3f7
  at: '2026-08-25T05:45:37Z'  # a3f7
  by: agent  # a3f7
  actor: jgstern_agent  # a3f7
  clock: 6  # a3f7
  nonce: a3f7  # a3f7
  message: "SATISFIED 2026-08-25, on the merged tree, judged against THIS ITEM'S OWN\nSTATEMENT -- \"every unresolved call edge emitted for a call ON A RECEIVER\ncarries meta['call_construct'] = 'method'\". Two PRs, because Phase 0 found the\ndefect was in two arms and wider than this item said.\n\nPR #521 -- RECEIVER EXPRESSIONS, java + scala + swift. call_construct was\nderived from the receiver's NAME, and the name is None for every receiver\nEXPRESSION. A/B on six real repositories, 82,001 call edges, arms differing in\none hunk, arm proved by module path AND by behaviour:\n    edge count      IDENTICAL in every repo -- nothing gained or lost\n    +15,896 edges   gained the stamp (19.4% of all call edges lacked it)\n     2,314 edges    changed dst (scala/swift routing; java 0)\nThe 2,314 are removals of confidently-wrong bindings, and the shape says so\nwithout sampling: 1,200 (51.9%) pointed at a field / variable / property --\nnot callable at all -- and 75% crossed a file boundary on nothing but a short\nname (.map() -> an unrelated package.map, .tearDown() -> a different test\nclass's, .field -> a data field).\n\nPR #522 -- EXPLICIT `this`, java only. My own scoping rationale in #521 was\nWRONG and is recorded here rather than quietly dropped: I excluded `this`\nbecause \"its receiver type IS known (the enclosing class), so there is no want\nof receiver evidence.\" An edge reaches that emit BECAUSE the call did not\nresolve, and a this.m() that does not resolve is one whose m is NOT on the\nenclosing class. Measured harm with a control in the same run:\n\n    this.doFinal(plain), no stamp   -> registers javax.crypto.Cipher.doFinal\n                                       as a barrier on its caller\n    same edge, stamped 'method'     -> NO BARRIER REGISTERED\n    correctly-typed receiver        -> still binds  (permit branch alive)\n    wrongly-typed receiver          -> refused      (permit branch not widened)\n\nSince PR #214 a barrier earns `sanitized`, which DROPS the flow from the\nclaim's violation set -- so the missing stamp deleted findings.\n    A/B, 218,049 java call edges: +85 stamped, 0 dst moved, 0 resolution moved.\nSmall, and said to be small: the argument is the phantom barrier and the\nparity, not the volume. Parity is measured -- python, go, objc, rust, scala and\nswift ALL stamp an explicit this/self; java alone did not. Recall cost measured\ntoo: gate_named_entry returns None immediately on call_construct == 'method',\nso the stamp can only refuse a FUNCTION-kind hit, and java.yaml declares 139\nmethod-kind, 3 attribute-kind and NO function-kind entries.\n\nTHE STANDING GUARD, which is the part that outlives the two fixes.\ntest_disclosure_parity.py now has THREE registry-driven arms -- plain\nidentifier receiver, receiver EXPRESSION, explicit this/self -- each driven\nfrom the shipped io_primitives catalogues, so a language arriving later with\nmethod-kind sinks and no fixture FAILS instead of going silently blind. Each\nexemption is a measurement with its own assertion: NO_EXTERNAL_CALL_EDGE\n{kotlin, javascript} (WI-nasuf) and COMPLEX_RECEIVER_NO_EDGE {python}\n(WI-makij, cited not re-filed). The consequence is pinned separately in\nhypergumbo-core (test_this_receiver_phantom_barrier.py), because a stamp test\nthat passes while the guard it feeds is inert is the exact shape that made\nWI-sajis necessary.\n\nTHE TITLE WAS DOUBLY FALSE and is left as filed for the record: WI-sajis\nrefuted \"java sets call_construct on no unresolved edge\" and Phase 0 refuted\n\"for Java at all\" -- the same defect was live in scala and swift.\n\nRESIDUAL, FILED NOT FOLDED. A java BARE unresolved call (implicit `this`)\nstill reaches the permit branch. That is a different statement, not this one\nhalf-done: this item says \"a call ON A RECEIVER\", and a bare call has no\nreceiver token. Stamping it would file a resolution fact under a SYNTACTIC\nconstruct name (ADR-0024, audit-findings 0012) -- the leak the concept audits\nexist to prevent -- so it needs a signal of its own. Filed separately.\n\nEvidence: ~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/campaign_p0_08252026/\n"  # a3f7
